{"title":"Twin Prime Conjecture","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The <b>Twin Prime Conjecture</b> is a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Conjecture\" title=\"Conjecture\">conjecture</a> (i.e., not a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Theorem\" title=\"Theorem\">theorem</a>) that states that there are <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Infinite\" title=\"Infinite\">infinitely</a> many pairs of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Twin_prime\" title=\"Twin prime\">twin primes</a>, i.e. pairs of primes that differ by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. It is one of the four problems collectively known as <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Landau%27s_Problems\" title=\"Landau&#39;s Problems\">Landau's Problems</a>.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Failed_Proofs\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Failed Proofs</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Using_an_infinite_series\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Using an infinite series</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Yitang_Zhang_approach\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Yitang Zhang approach</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Alternative_statements\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Alternative statements</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Failed_Proofs\">Failed Proofs</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Twin_Prime_Conjecture&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Failed Proofs\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Using_an_infinite_series\">Using an infinite series</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Twin_Prime_Conjecture&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Using an infinite series\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>One possible strategy to prove the infinitude of twin primes is an idea adopted from the proof of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Dirichlet%27s_Theorem\" title=\"Dirichlet&#39;s Theorem\">Dirichlet's Theorem</a>.  If one can show that the sum \n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/2/a12e076b39c41c67669c21ae7404809000ed1a6e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{7}+\\frac{1}{11}+\\frac{1}{13}+\\frac{1}{17}+\\frac{1}{19}+\\cdots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"384\" height=\"38\" /></center>\n<p>of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Reciprocal\" title=\"Reciprocal\">reciprocals</a> of twin primes <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Diverge&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Diverge (page does not exist)\">diverges</a>, this would imply that there are infinitely many twin primes.  Unfortunately, it has been shown that this sum converges to a constant <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, known as <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Brun%27s_constant\" title=\"Brun&#39;s constant\">Brun's constant</a>.  This could mean either that there are <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Finite\" title=\"Finite\">finitely</a> many twin prime pairs or that they are spaced \"too far apart\" for that <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Series\" title=\"Series\">series</a> to diverge.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Yitang_Zhang_approach\">Yitang Zhang approach</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Twin_Prime_Conjecture&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Yitang Zhang approach\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>A weaker version of twin prime conjecture was proved by Yitang Zhang in 2013.\nThis version stated that there are infinitely many pairs of primes that differ by a finite number. The number Yitang chose was 7,000,000.  Terence Tao and other people have reduced that boundary to 246 more numbers.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Alternative_statements\">Alternative statements</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Twin_Prime_Conjecture&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Alternative statements\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>One alternative statement of the Twin Prime Conjecture, is that there exists infinitely many natural numbers not of forms: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/2/3f26a5abbab6f48b0c4ff609cd63cef48ee2086c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[6ab+a+b,6ab+a-b,6ab-a+b,6ab-a-b\\]\" width=\"377\" height=\"16\" /> with natural number inputs greater than 0.  Because, letting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> be of one of these forms one of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/9/c29996efce026fe145a53edb2f232368829703a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6n\\pm 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"50\" height=\"13\" /> factors so only if one of variables is 0 will the factorization be trivial (contain only 1 and itself).\n</p><p>Another is that there are infinitely many values <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/e/c8ebe98ce1cdf53de05029df5610b10bed681ed8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12m$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" /> that have goldbach partitions of distance from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> of 1. \n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Twin_Prime_Conjecture&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017191155\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.015 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.029 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 59/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 304/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 1494/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.394      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.394      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:2087:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017191155 and revision id 238208. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}